B. PTIME Solution and Why Not to Use It
Theorem 1 (PTIME solution) .The MILP program in Equa-
tion (2)can be solved in time complexity that is polynomial
in the number of input tuples in R.
Proof. The main proof idea is to replace the indicator vari-
ables in Equation (2) one-by-one, until the MILP program
is converted to a polynomial number of LP programs, i.e.,
linear programs without integer variables. Since LP can be
solved in time that is polynomial in the number of variables
and constraints, solving a polynomial number of LP problems
yields polynomial time complexity overall.
For the LP reduction, consider a case where we have
assigned a fixed value—either 0 or 1—to each indicator
δsr, r∈Rπ(k), r̸=s∈R. Then Equation (2) reduces to
the LP problem of returning any (w1, . . . , w m)that satisfies
