i=1wi(s.Ai−r.Ai)≤ε2,
δsr= 0, r∈Rπ(k), r̸=s∈R(4)

Intuitively, when fixing the value of δsr, its 2 indicator
constraints in Equation (2) are replaced by the corresponding
inequality. Since there are no integer variables in Equation (4),
we are left with an LP problem with mvariables and kn
constraints, which can be solved in time polynomial in m, kn .
Generating all 2kncombinations of 0/1 assignments to
theknindicators produces an exponential number of LP
problems. We avoid this by exploiting a well-known result
from computational geometry that the number of distinct
partitions generated by xhyperplanes in d-dimensional space
isO(xd)[44]. Note that for each indicator δsr, the corre-
sponding hyperplane is defined by the score-difference formula∑︁m
i=1wi(s.Ai−r.Ai) = 0 . Since our problem has kn
indicators and mdimensions, the number of possible partitions
is limited to O(kmnm), which is polynomial in n, the number
of tuples in R.3
In order to generate all distinct partitions defined by the kn
hyperplanes in time O(kmnm), we can use standard depth-first
search (DFS) with backtracking or breadth-first search (BFS).
To see how the algorithm works, consider a node Nin the
corresponding tree of states and let Idenote the conjunction
of all inequalities of type∑︁m
i=1wi(x.Ai−y.Ai)≥ε1or∑︁m
i=1wi(x.Ai−y.Ai)≤ε2, depending on the value of each
indicator δxythe algorithm has branched on along the path
from the root to node N. To expand from N, the algorithm
picks one of the remaining indicators, say δrs. It checks the
relationship between N’s feasible region (defined by I) and
the hyperplanes H1=∑︁m
i=1wi(s.Ai−r.Ai)≥ε1andH0=∑︁m
i=1wi(s.Ai−r.Ai)≤ε2forδrs. Formally, the algorithm
first checks if I ∧H 0is satisfiable. If so, it adds a child node
C0toN, whose feasible region constraint is I ∧ H 0. Then
it analogously checks I ∧ H 1and, if satisfiable, adds another
child node C1with feasible region constraint I ∧ H 1. Both
satisfiability checks are LP programs (no integer variables)
and hence take polynomial time. Notice that since H0∧H 1is
unsatisfiable because ε1> ε 2, the algorithm cannot encounter
the same partition more than once. And due to the O(kmnm)
upper bound on the number of possible partitions, the number
of nodes reached by the search algorithm is O(kmnm)as well.
(Intuitively, many nodes only have a single child, because their
feasible region lies completely above or completely below the
indicator’s hyperplane.)
Since the proof of Theorem 1 is constructive, we could use
the corresponding algorithm to solve OPT in time polynomial
in the number of tuples in R.While a guaranteed PTIME so-
lution may appear preferable compared to the theoretically
NP-hard world of MILP, we argue that holistically solving
Equation (2) with an MILP solver is the better approach.
This hypothesis is confirmed by our empirical evaluation in
Section VI-B. Modern MILP solvers already rely on advanced
backtracking algorithms that allow them to use results from
one part of the search space to rule out the existence of
