solutions in other parts of the search space. And these tech-
niques often lead to significant improvements compared to
simple backtracking [45], where sub-problems for the different
partitions defined by the hyperplanes are fed piece-meal to
separate LP instances. In short, the PTIME approach in the
proof of Theorem 1, as well as similar techniques for related
ranking problems [31], [36], correspond to simple algorithms
that lack the clever cross-branch information passing and other
sophisticated strategies employed by modern MILP solvers.
IV. S YMBOLIC GRADIENT DESCENT
We introduce S YM-GD and discuss its design decisions.
A.SYM-GD Overview and Algorithm
SYM-GD, like the well-known gradient-descent approaches,
starts from a seed point, here an initial weight vector W0,
and then moves toward a local minimum (of ranking approx-
imation error). However, it is more like “gradient descent on
steroids”: Instead of moving toward a local optimum, S YM-
GD actually finds the true local optimum within a cell of size
caround the seed point. This means that it does not require a
differentiable error function, but it needs an appropriate solver,
such as an MILP solver in the case of OPT. Upon finding
the local optimum, the cell shifts accordingly and the process
continues in the new neighborhood. Algorithm 1 summarizes
the basic idea. Function solve( W, c) runs the MILP solver for
Equation (2), with the additional constraints that Wfalls into
the cell of size caround seed point W:max( wi−c/2,0)≤
wi≤min(wi+c/2,1)fori∈ {1, . . . , m },0< c < 2.
SYM-GD exploits the structure of Equation (2), which
ensures that execution time drops dramatically as cell size
decreases . Intuitively, the smaller a cell in weight space, the
fewer of the indicator hyperplanes∑︁m
i=1wi(s.Ai−r.Ai) =ε1
and∑︁m
i=1wi(s.Ai−r.Ai) = ε2intersect it, meaning the
corresponding indicator value is uniquely determined and
hence the indicator becomes a constant. In the extreme, for
a cell not intersected by any of the hyperplanes, Equation (2)
reduces to the LP problem in Equation (4). Stated differently,
in a sufficiently small cell, the complexity of OPT drops
by a factor of O(kmnm), which can be several orders of
magnitude for large input.
B. How to Select the Seed Point?
RANK HOW currently supports 2 main strategies for se-
lecting seed points. The first relies on a fast heuristic like
standard linear regression or ordinal regression, which both

